<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Galicia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as New Galicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was the name of the Polish lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> and Radom, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> annexed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795. Austria lost the province by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> in 1809 to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, regaining only the Kraków region later, in 1846.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-galicia.jpg" alt="West Galicia" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Galicia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> lay north of the Kingdom of Galicia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, the lands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had taken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> of 1772. It covered the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> to the outskirts of Praga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, with the Bug and the Pilica and their tributaries forming its northern border with New East Prussia. From 1797 its government, the Gubernium, sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. The province was divided into twelve districts, among them Kraków, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a>, Radom, Kielce, Sandomierz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a>, Siedlce and Biała Podlaska.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a> of 1794, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> agreed with Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to divide what remained of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and so abolish it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> joined the agreement on 24 October 1795. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, which had taken no part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a>, received this share. In 1803 New Galicia was merged into the Kingdom of Galicia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, while keeping some autonomy. It remained Austrian in 1807, when Napoleon I created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> from lands Prussia gave up under the Treaty of Tilsit.</p><p>The end came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a>. On 15 April 1809 a corps under Archduke Ferdinand Karl Joseph of Austria-Este invaded the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, beginning the Polish–Austrian War. The archduke had hoped to enter as a national liberator, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Prince Józef Poniatowski</a>'s forces met him at the Battle of Raszyn. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> on 6 July, the province and the adjoining District of Zamosc passed to the Duchy of Warsaw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A civil code of its own was introduced in West Galicia before the Austrian Civil Code of 1811; it was based on natural law and did little to resolve the problems of feudal society. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/final-act-of-the-congress-of-vienna">Final Act of the Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 most of the former province became part of Congress Poland, ruled in personal union by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> became the nominally independent Free City of Kraków.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Galicia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conquistadors spread out to subdue Oaxaca, Michoacán, Guatemala and the Gulf coast, while Nuño de Guzmán’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of <b>New Galicia</b> in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>New Galicia</b> had its own audiencia from 1548, seated in Guadalajara from 1560, giving it a degree of autonomy within New Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-new-galicia">Kingdom of New Galicia</a></li></ul></section>
